        "description": "## Stability New releases come out many times a day, but the install command below gives you the **stable channel**: a release reaches it only after the full test suite and a two-hour, 600,000-page load test pass on that exact release, and only when an important fix is waiting or stable is a week old. Updates are never installed without your say-so unless you turn on automatic upgrades. After an upgrade, gbrain shows a one-time notice listing what changed in behaviour. Stable also comes with a written promise about which commands, flags, tools, config keys and output fields stay the same, and how much warning you get before one changes. docs/STABILITY.md explains that promise, how often stable moves, how to pin a version or go back, and what the faster alpha channel is for. ## Install > [!TIP] > **Fastest: hosted GBrain at gbrain.io, for any harness.** One command launches a GBrain hosted on gbrain.io; Claude Code, Codex, ChatGPT, Cursor and every other AI you use connect to it, with nothing to keep online. > > ```bash > curl -sSL https://gbrainio.terminalwire.sh | bash > gbrainio terminal connect # in a new terminal > gbrainio workspace new # pick a plan in the browser > curl -fsSL https://bun.sh/install | bash # skip if Bun 1.4.4+ is installed > export PATH=\"$HOME/.bun/bin:$PATH\" > bun install -g github:garrytan/gbrain#stable # the gbrain CLI only; no local brain > # Claude Code (an existing gbrain entry is kept): > (claude mcp get gbrain >/dev/null 2>&1 || claude mcp add --transport http --scope user gbrain https://gbrain.io/mcp) && gbrain setup claude-code --instructions-only > # Codex (an existing gbrain entry is kept): > (codex mcp get gbrain >/dev/null 2>&1 || codex mcp add gbrain --url https://gbrain.io/mcp) && gbrain setup codex --instructions-only > ``` > > `gbrain setup --instructions-only` tells the agent the brain is there; agents seldom call an MCP server nobody told them about. It writes the brain-first block into `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` or Codex's `AGENTS.md` (a non-empty `AGENTS.override.md` instead when Codex has one) with a per-turn rule hook, and leaves the MCP entry alone; the same command with `--no-instructions` added takes them out. > > The installer is a shell script for macOS and Linux; read it before you run it. ### Here's how to self-host it GBrain is MIT-licensed and free to self-host. It needs **Bun 1.4.4 or newer**; existing worker installs read the authorization and queue upgrade guide before restarting on this version. > [!WARNING] > **GBrain is NOT distributed on npm.** The npm `gbrain` package is unrelated and > can shadow the real binary, so never run `npm install -g gbrain` or > `bun add -g gbrain`. Install only via `bun install -g github:garrytan/gbrain#stable` > (the soaked stable channel; `#latest-stable` is the alpha line with every release) > or `git clone --branch stable` + `bun install && bun link`. Installed by mistake? `gbrain doctor` gives the fix. > Schema not migrated (#218)? Run > `gbrain apply-migrations --yes --no-autopilot-install`; if it exits 1, run `gbrain doctor --json`, not another `--yes` (`wedged`: relay its fix). ```bash bun install -g github:garrytan/gbrain#stable gbrain setup claude-code              # or: gbrain setup codex gbrain init --pglite --no-embedding   # any other harness, or no agent ``` `gbrain setup` shows what it will write first (`--dry-run` previews, `--remove` undoes), creates a keyless brain only if none exists, wires MCP, hooks and a brain-first block in the agent's instruction file (`--no-instructions` skips it), and smoke-tests them (setup guide). For any other coding agent, paste: ```text Add GBrain memory to this existing agent. Read and follow: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/garrytan/gbrain/master/INSTALL_FOR_AGENTS.md Keep my current identity and instructions, start keyless, and show me the search-mode choice. Verify a remember/recall round trip with real GBrain calls. ... Postgres, thin clients, API keys and upgrades: docs/INSTALL.md. Agents: INSTALL_FOR_AGENTS.md. Memory-only walkthrough: give your coding agent a memory.",
